papers.abstract:
In this paper we aim to study in a unified fashion existence and regularity properties of solutions to a very general class of Dirichlet boundary value problems such as \begin{equation}\label{P1abs}\tag{1}
  \begin{cases}
  - \mbox{div}\left(\displaystyle \phi(|\nabla u|) \nabla u\right) = f(x) &\quad \mbox{in } \Omega,\newline
  u = 0 &\quad \mbox{on } \partial \Omega,
  \end{cases} \end{equation} where $\phi:[0,\infty)\to [0,\infty)$ is any function such that $\phi(s)s \in C((0,\infty))$ is a non-decreasing function which tends to $1$ as $s\to\infty$. Among others, most of the far-famed operators derived from functionals with linear growth, as for instance the $1$-Laplacian and the minimal surface operator are included in our model. Under suitable assumptions on the data we prove existence and regularity results for problem \eqref{P1abs} which recover the sharp thresholds known in the model cases.

It is a classical fact that random walk on the infinite supercritical Bernoulli $p$-percolation cluster on $\mathbb{Z}^{d}$ converges to Brownian motion with some diffusivity $\sigma(p)$, and that the graph metric on the infinite cluster has order $\mu(p) \|\cdot\|$ at large scales for some constant $\mu(p)$ known as the time constant. We compute the near-critical asymptotics of the two quantities in high dimensions, showing that
  \begin{align*}
  \sigma^2(p) \asymp (p-p_c)^2 \qquad \text{ and } \qquad \mu(p) \asymp \frac{1}{\sqrt{p-p_c}}
  \end{align*} for all $p>p_c$. Our results concerning the diffusivity confirm a conjecture of Biskup (2011). We also compute the asymptotic critical probabilities of slabs in the same regime, showing that $p_c( \mathbb{Z}^{d-1} \times \{0,\ldots,n\}) - p_c(\mathbb{Z}^{d}) \asymp n^{-2}$ as $n\to \infty$.

In this paper, we investigate oscillation and non-oscillation results of second order differential equations
  \begin{equation}
  f''+(H_1e^z+H_2e^{\alpha z}+q)f=0,\tag{+}
  \end{equation} where $H_1,H_2\in\C\setminus\{0\}$, $0<\alpha<1$, and $q$ is an entire function of order less than $1$. First, we show that if equation (+) has a zero-free non-trivial entire solution, then either $\alpha=1/2$ or $\alpha=3/4$, which settles the Problem 9 posed in \cite{HITW}. Second, we prove that if either $q$ is transcendental, or $\alpha=(2m-1)/(2m)$ for some integer $m\geq 2$ and $q\equiv 0$, then every non-trivial entire solution $f$ of $(+)$ satisfies $\lambda(f)=\infty$.

We study adversarial bandits with switching costs. For $K$ arms, $T$ rounds, unit switching costs, and oblivious losses in $[0,1]$, we prove that the minimax regret is \begin{equation*}
  \Theta\!\left(\min\left\{T,
  \frac{K^{1/3}T^{2/3}}{\log^{2/3}(2+T/K)}\right\}\right)\,. \end{equation*} This shows that simple blocking, which plays one arm throughout each block, is suboptimal. Our algorithm makes at most a constant number of switches at random times within each block. The key idea for the improvement is to extract more information per switch by giving greater weight to observations near switching times. We also introduce detours, during which the learner temporarily plays a newly sampled arm. Larger observed losses make a detour more likely. These detours cancel temporal correlation terms induced by blocking while preserving the observations used in the estimates. A matching lower bound follows from a refined analysis of a modified multiscale random walk construction.

In this paper, we study positive solutions to the problem
  \begin{equation*}
  -\Delta u = |x|^{-s_2}u^{2^*(s_2)-1} - \la |x|^{-s_1}u^{p-1} \quad \text{in }\Om, \qquad u=0 \quad \text{on }\partial\Om,
  \end{equation*}
  where $\Om\subset\R^N$ ($N\ge 3$) is a bounded domain with $0\in\partial\Om$, $0\le s_1<s_2<2$, $2^*(s)=\frac{2(N-s)}{N-2}$, $2^*(s_2)<p<2^*(s_1)$, and $\la>0$. This is the Li--Lin open problem \cite{LL} in the form studied by Cerami, Zhong, and Zou \cite{CZZ} and by Tang and Tang \cite{TT1}. Assume that $\partial\Om$ is $C^{1,\alpha}$ near $0$ with a leading term of homogeneity $1+\alpha$, and set $\theta=\frac{(N-2)(2^*(s_1)-p)}{2}$. We prove that a solution exists for every $\la>0$ if $\theta>\alpha$ and the $\alpha$-mean curvature of $\partial\Om$ at $0$ is negative, whereas $\la^*=\sup\{\la>0:\text{a solution exists}\}$ is finite if $\theta\le\alpha$, without any curvature assumption. For $\alpha=1$, this removes the restriction to almost every $\la$ in \cite[Theorem 1.6]{CZZ}, answers a question of Zou \cite{Z}, and disproves the conjecture in \cite[Remark 1.7]{TT1}. The proofs rely on a scale-invariant estimate uniform in $\la$, a Hadamard-type identity for the half-space extremal and a localized Pohozaev identity combined with sharp decay estimates. Notably, no regularity of $\partial\Om$ is required away from $0$.
